Title: Diagnosis
Details: The test case `testMath221` asserts that multiplying the complex number (0 + i) by (-1 + 0i) should yield (0 - i). According to complex number multiplication rules, indeed (0 + i) * (-1 + 0i) = (0 - i). 

Inspecting the multiply method implementation:

```java
public Complex multiply(Complex rhs) {
    if (isNaN() || rhs.isNaN()) {
        return NaN;
    }
    if (Double.isInfinite(real) || Double.isInfinite(imaginary) ||
        Double.isInfinite(rhs.real)|| Double.isInfinite(rhs.imaginary)) {
        // we don't use Complex.isInfinite() to avoid testing for NaN again
        return INF;
    }
    return createComplex(real * rhs.real - imaginary * rhs.imaginary,
            real * rhs.imaginary + imaginary * rhs.real);
}
```

This implementation follows the correct formula for multiplying two complex numbers (a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i. This logic appears correct.

The equals method:

```java
public boolean equals(Object other) {
    boolean ret;

    if (this == other) { 
        ret = true;
    } else if (other == null) {
        ret = false;
    } else  {
        try {
            Complex rhs = (Complex)other;
            if (rhs.isNaN()) {
                ret = this.isNaN();
            } else {
                ret = (Double.doubleToRawLongBits(real) == Double.doubleToRawLongBits(rhs.getReal())) && 
                      (Double.doubleToRawLongBits(imaginary) == Double.doubleToRawLongBits(rhs.getImaginary())); 
            }
        } catch (ClassCastException ex) {
            // ignore exception
            ret = false;
        }
    }

    return ret;
}
```

This equality method compares the real and imaginary parts using exact raw bits comparison. This can cause issues due to floating point precision – small rounding errors in multiplication might make two theoretically equal complex numbers not exactly bitwise equal.

The bug probably comes from floating-point rounding inaccuracies during the multiplication step—when multiplying (0 + i) and (-1 + 0i), the return values might have very small rounding errors causing the equals method to return false.

Therefore, the root cause of the bug is likely that equals uses exact bitwise equality on double fields rather than a more tolerant comparison allowing for floating point epsilon differences. The multiply method correctly implements multiplication but does not mitigate floating point inaccuracies in the result.

Summary:  
- multiply uses correct mathematical formula  
- equals uses strict bitwise equality that fails if there are minor floating point deviations  
- test expects (0 - i), but actual equals comparison fails due to precision differences in the multiplication result  

This diagnosis matches the bug report about multiply and equals returning wrong result after multiplication of complex numbers.

